 Portfolio 1: Database Design (NairaPay)

 Case study
NairaPay is a simulated digital bank in Nigeria. Users: customers, bank tellers and auditors. Major data: customers, accounts, deposits, withdrawals, transfers and transaction logs. Key operations: transfer funds, reverse failed transactions, and audit account balances.

 ER diagram
![ER diagram](portfolio-1-database-design/er-diagram.png)

 Schema
The tables are created by schema.sql: customers, accounts and transactions.
- Every table has a primary key.
- accounts.customer_id links each account to a customer (foreign key).
- transactions.from_account_id and to_account_id link each transaction to accounts (foreign keys).
- CHECK rules stop negative balances and zero or negative amounts. UNIQUE stops duplicate emails and account numbers.

 Normalisation

 The unnormalised table
| txn_id | account_number | customer_name | customer_email | account_type | balance | amount |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 1000000001 | Ada Obi | ada@mail.com | savings | 95000 | 5000 |
| 2 | 1000000001 | Ada Obi | ada@mail.com | savings | 95000 | 20000 |
| 3 | 1000000002 | Musa Bello | musa@mail.com | current | 105000 | 5000 |

Problems with this table:
- Update anomaly: Ada's email is repeated on every row, so changing it means editing many rows, and missing one leaves contradictory data.
- Insert anomaly: a customer who has opened an account but made no transaction has no row to be stored in.
- Delete anomaly: deleting a customer's only transaction also deletes the customer's details.

 1NF
Every cell holds one value and there are no repeating groups, so the table is already in 1NF.
 
 2NF
The key is txn_id + account_number. The columns account_type, balance and the customer details depend only on account_number, not on the whole key. I moved them into an accounts table. The transactions table keeps txn_id, account_number and amount.

 3NF
customer_name and customer_email describe the person, not the account. I moved them into a customers table and linked it to accounts with customer_id.

 Conclusion
My three tables (customers, accounts, transactions) are in 3NF. Each has a primary key, each cell holds one value, and every column describes only the subject of its own table. Ada's email is now stored once, so changing it updates one row.
